solution_md:
Answer:  $\boxed D$  $f(2)=5 \cdot 2^{11} - 2\cdot 2^5 - 3\cdot 2 = 5\cdot 2 \cdot 1024 - 64 - 6 = 10240 - 70 = 10170 = 2\cdot 3^2 \cdot 5 \cdot 113$ .

We will investigate whether the numbers  $2$ ,  $9$ ,  $5$ , and  $113$  always divide  $f(n)$ . 

It is easy to see that  $f(n)$  is an even number.

From Euler's theorem, \(n^5 \equiv n \pmod{5}\), so \(f(n) = 5n^{11} - 2n^5 - 3n \equiv -2n - 3n \equiv 0 \pmod{5}\).

When \(n \equiv 0 \pmod{3}\), \(9\) divides \(f(n)\).
When \(n \equiv 1, 2 \pmod{3}\), \((n, 9) = 1\) and from Euler's theorem, \(n^{\varphi (9)} = n^6 \equiv 1 \pmod{9}\). Then \(f(n) = 5n^{11} - 2n^5 - 3n \equiv 5n^5 - 2n^5 - 3n \equiv 3n(n^4-1) \pmod{9}\). Since \(n^4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}\), it follows that \(9\) divides \(f(n)\).

Since \(2\), \(9\), and \(5\) are always divisors, the answer depends on whether \(113\) is a divisor. If \(113\) is a divisor, the answer is \(2 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 = 12\), otherwise it's \(2 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 = 24\).

Since \(24\) isn't included among the options, the answer is \(\boxed{12}\).

It would be a bit bold for \(113\) to be a divisor of this kind of number. However, we can show that it's not a divisor with some calculations and a counterexample: $
\begin{array}{lcll}
f(3) & = & 5 \cdot 3^{11} - 2 \cdot 3^5 - 3 \cdot 3 & \pmod{113} 
& \equiv & 5 \cdot 3^5 \cdot 3^5 \cdot 3 - 2 \cdot 3^5 - 9 & \pmod{113} 
& \equiv & 17 \cdot (15 \cdot 17 - 2) - 9 & \pmod{113} 
& \equiv & 17 \cdot 27 - 9 & \pmod{113} 
& \equiv & 450 & \pmod{113} 
& \equiv & -2 & \pmod{113}
\end{array}
$

