First, let me give you a nice problem:

<details><summary>Problem</summary>Suppose that you have a table of  $2^n \times 2^n$  cells with one cell removed from the table. Then you can cover your table with such shapes: [asy]
unitsize(11);
draw((0,0)--(0,2));
draw((1,0)--(1,2));
draw((2,1)--(2,2));
draw((0,0)--(1,0));
draw((0,1)--(2,1));
draw((0,2)--(2,2));[/asy]

<details><summary>Spoiler</summary>Use induction on  $n$  and divide the table to 4 equal parts.</details></details>

Now, come back to our problem. Let  $x$  be the number of shapes with three cells and let  $y$  be the number of shapes with  $4$  cells. So we have  $3x + 4y = 22$ . Solving this, you get  $(x,y)=(6,1)$ , or  $(2,4)$ . Checking the answer  $(6, 1)$  you can easily draw this:
[asy]
