for(int i=0; i<6; ++i)
{
if(i<5)
draw( (i, 0)--(i,5) );
else draw( (i, 0)--(i,2));
if(i < 3)
draw((0,i)--(5,i));
else draw((0,i)--(4,i));
}
draw((2,0)--(5,0),red+ linewidth(2));
draw((5,0)--(5,2),red+ linewidth(2));
draw((4,2)--(5,2), red+linewidth(2));
draw((4,1)--(4,2),red+ linewidth(2));
draw((2,1)--(4,1), red+linewidth(2));
draw((2,0)--(2,1), red+linewidth(2));
fill(shift(0,0 )*unitsquare);
fill(shift(1,0 )*unitsquare);
fill(shift(0,1 )*unitsquare);
[/asy]
Now, you have a  $4\times 4$  square with one cell removed and according to the  above problem, you can cover it with those three cells shapes. So the answer is  $6$ .

