Note that 
\[
a_{n+1}^2 + b_{n+1}^2 = \bigl(\alpha^2+\beta^2\bigr)\bigl(a_n^2+b_n\bigr),
\]
hence  $\alpha^2+\beta^2=1$ . Reparameterizing  $(\alpha,\beta)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$  for  $0\le \theta\le \pi/2$ , and then casting the system in matrix form, we arrive at
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
a_{n+1} b_{n+1}
\end{pmatrix}
=\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \sin\theta &\cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}}_{\triangleq R_\theta}\begin{pmatrix}
a_{n} b_{n}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Clearly,  $R_\theta$  is a rotation, and it is easily seen that  $R_{\theta_1}R_{\theta_2}=R_{\theta_1+\theta_2}$  for every  $\theta_1,\theta_2\in\mathbb{R}$ . Having done the math, we arrive at  $(a_{1997},b_{1997})=(b_1,a_1)$  iff 
\[
\cos(1997\theta)=\sin(\theta)\qquad\text{and}\qquad \sin(1997\theta)=\cos(\theta).
\]
The rest is easy.

